+## Container provenance versus claim provenance
+
+Every material claim has two potentially different provenance layers:
+
+1. `Source container provenance`
+   - how the containing text, checkpoint, note, transcript, or report entered the current input;
+2. `Claim provenance`
+   - the epistemic origin of the underlying assertion itself.
+
+A container can be exact current content while its nested claims remain prior-derived.
+
+Example:
+
+```text
+PRIOR_SUMMARY:
+Revision abc1234 passed 18 tests.
+```
+
+Correct classification:
+
+- `Source container provenance`: `EXACT_CONTENT_CURRENT_INPUT`;
+- `Claim provenance`: `PRIOR_CHECKPOINT_DERIVED`;
+- `Current raw support`: `NO`;
+- `Independent verification in this checkpoint`: `NO`.
+
+The current presence of a prior summary verifies that the summary text was supplied. It does not upgrade the claims inside that summary.
+
+Apply this rule to:
+
+- `PRIOR_SUMMARY` blocks;
+- `BEGIN_PREVIOUS_CHECKPOINT` content;
+- pasted checkpoint summaries;
+- retrospective project summaries;
+- clean-chat checkpoint intake;
+- notes explicitly quoting or attributing a claim to an earlier summary or checkpoint;
+- repeated project history whose only stated source is an earlier checkpoint.
+
+When exact prior-checkpoint wording matters, optionally use:
+
+- `Nested claim representation`: `PRIOR_CHECKPOINT_EXACT_QUOTE`.
+
+Otherwise use:
+
+- `Claim provenance`: `PRIOR_CHECKPOINT_DERIVED`.
+
+If current raw evidence independently corroborates the same claim, preserve both paths:
+
+```text
+Claim provenance: PRIOR_CHECKPOINT_DERIVED
+Current corroborating provenance: RAW_CURRENT_INPUT
+Current raw support: YES
+```
+
+Do not replace the prior-derived origin with the corroborating source.
+
